          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-6a5c86748129f326ccce252b",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-efdab7152b3fdd66611ea135",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-efdab7152b3fdd66611ea135",
          "source_text_sha256": "49c250abf2e8d739bc18dd827a36f509216a3a4bfd02f398f53135faee3a02b5",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On July 29, 2024, at approximately 6:30 a.m., two witnesses were outdoors in the commune of Échirolles (38). They observed to the west a white glowing point moving at a slow and steady speed, following a south-north trajectory at a constant elevation of around 60°. No noise was perceived during the observation. After 5 minutes, the phenomenon disappeared towards the northwest, obscured by the surrounding vegetation. The consistency was deemed satisfactory, although only one of the two witnesses completed a questionnaire. A 44-second video of the phenomenon, captured by T1, was recovered and analyzed for the investigation.\n\nAccording to GEIPAN criteria, consistency refers to the amount of reliable and objective information gathered for a testimony. Two hypotheses were considered: that of an airplane and that of a balloon, more specifically, a cluster of balloons. The airplane hypothesis, which initially presented similarities with PAN (elongated shape resembling a fuselage, common white color, steady and straight-line movement), proved implausible, mainly due to the absence of such an aircraft on radar maps. The cluster of balloon hypothesis is more plausible, based on the following elements:\n\n- Overall elongated shape and presence of elements within this shape resembling individual round balloons.\n- White color, common for balloons, and bright, as they were illuminated by the rising sun.\n- Slow, steady, and straight-line movement, consistent with the behavior of wind-carried balloons.\n- Measured and calculated data from the video are compatible for a set of wind-carried balloons, approximately 80 cm to 1 meter long, located between about 40 meters and 70 meters from the witnesses.\n\nWind data show it was highly variable, with a shear phenomenon at all levels between ground level and 500 meters altitude, and across a wide sector in the direction of observation. This phenomenon is visible in the fine-mesh AROME* data restitution, which model can sometimes differ from real-time data, possibly the case here. Therefore, the orientation of the south-to-north wind in the balloons' movement sector is possible but not demonstrable.\n\nThe GEIPAN classifies this case as \"B\": probable observation of a cluster of balloons.\n\nAROME (Application of Research to Operations at MEsoscale) is a model with a very fine mesh (1.3 km mesh) for forecasting in France (METEO France).",
          "translation_text_sha256": "08293d6ec2b58d1ce0a99415a0effa876a3bd4da4f7972e971675050672932a2"
        }
      ],
